    "observations": [
      "Running `python3 app.py` times out, outputting 'TimeoutError: deadline 5ms elapsed before response at 120ms' (exit code 1).",
      "config.json sets 'request_timeout_ms' to 5 (milliseconds), and 'simulated_response_ms' to 120 (milliseconds).",
      "The simulated downstream response latency is constant at 120ms, but the timeout is set to only 5ms, so the simulated request always fails.",
      "Manually passing --timeout-ms 5000 makes the app succeed, reporting 'HTTP 200: healthy' (exit code 0)."
    ],
    "outcome": "reproduced",
    "reproduced_process_ids": [
      "proc-305f1ec4fd77e83f6df04e7b",
      "proc-cbd6a5557880907a2a3fc576"
    ],
    "reproduction_steps": [
      "Run `python3 app.py` (using config.json) and observe timeout error and exit code 1.",
      "Run `python3 app.py --timeout-ms 5000` (or with both --config config.json and --timeout-ms 5000) and observe healthy result and exit code 0."
    ],
    "root_cause": {
      "evidence_process_ids": [
        "proc-305f1ec4fd77e83f6df04e7b",
        "proc-cbd6a5557880907a2a3fc576"
      ],
      "statement": "request_timeout_ms in config.json is set to 5 (milliseconds), which is far less than the intended 5000 milliseconds (5 seconds) and insufficient for the 120ms simulated downstream latency, causing every request to time out."
    },
    "summary": "The failure was reproduced: every health check timed out because config.json's request_timeout_ms was accidentally set to 5 (ms) instead of the intended 5000 (ms). Overriding the timeout to 5000ms via command-line fixes the issue, as does editing config.json to match the intended timeout. The root cause is a misconfiguration: a too-low request_timeout_ms value."
  },
